==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Alternative forms===
* [[homoeostasis]] {{UK}}
* [[homœostasis]] {{qualifier|dated}}

===Etymology===
Coined from {{etyl|grc|en}} {{term|ὅμος||similar|tr=hómos|sc=polytonic|lang=grc}} + {{term|ιστημι||standing still|tr=histēmi|sc=polytonic|lang=grc}}/[[stasis]] (from {{term|στάσις|sc=polytonic|lang=grc}}) by {{w|Walter Bradford Cannon}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/ˌhoʊmioʊˈsteɪsɪs/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|homeostases}}

# {{context|physiology|lang=en}} The [[ability]] of a [[system]] or [[living]] [[organism]] to [[adjust]] its [[internal]] [[environment]] to maintain a state of dynamic constancy; such as the ability of [[warm-blooded]] animals to maintain a stable [[temperature]].
#* '''2011''', ''Professional Guide to Pathophysiology'', Lippincott, Williams, & Wilkins, page 1:
#*: When '''homeostasis''' is disrupted by an external stressor - such as injury, lack of nutrients, or invasion by parasites or other organisms - illness may occur.
# Such a dynamic equilibrium or balance.

